    "result_text": "I implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py` against the installed mcp 2.2.0. All 6 tests pass: the 3 existing ones plus 3 new ones for `run_tool`.\n\n```python\nresult = await session.call_tool(name, arguments)\ntext = \"\\n\".join(block.text for block in result.content if block.type == \"text\")\nif result.is_error:\n    raise ToolCallFailed(name, text)\nif result.structured_content is not None:\n    return result.structured_content\nreturn text\n```\n\n- **Field names:** in this version of mcp the result fields are `is_error` and `structured_content` (snake_case).\n- **Failures:** a failed call raises `ToolCallFailed` with the text blocks joined by `\"\\n\"`, even if the server also sent structured output.\n- **Structured output:** this can be any JSON value, including `0`, `[]` or `\"\"`. I check `is not None` so those are still returned as-is. Only a missing value falls back to text.\n- **Text blocks:** blocks that aren't text, like images, are skipped. I pick out text blocks by their `type` field, so there's no import from `mcp_types`, which `mcp` pulls in but doesn't re-export.\n\nThe 3 new tests are in `tests/test_scratch_run_tool.py`. They use a fake session to cover the text-only case (with an image block mixed in), structured output (including `0`), and failures. I meant that file to be temporary, but deleting it needed approval that I didn't get, so it's still there. Keep it as a regression test (maybe renamed to `test_calls.py`) or delete it."
